1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:21,839
This is Idaho sagebrush country. Very ordinary to the eye, yet a land of exotic names. The lost

2
00:00:21,839 --> 00:00:29,880
river country. East of the craters of the moon, the snake. A land of paradox and

3
00:00:29,880 --> 00:00:35,880
contradiction. Tied to the past by the lava flows of a million years ago. Yet

4
00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:42,640
tied to the future by the work that goes on now. The beaver trappers, the mountain

5
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:48,400
men, came through here over a century ago, learning the rivers and passes, making

6
00:00:48,399 --> 00:00:54,539
the maps of memory that enabled them to guide such Johnny-come-latelys as Fremont, and later

7
00:00:54,539 --> 00:01:02,119
on the wagon trains of pioneers who went that-a-way en route to Oregon and California.

8
00:01:02,119 --> 00:01:06,780
Those pioneers saw little reason to linger here.

9
00:01:06,780 --> 00:01:10,000
But that was yesterday.

10
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:17,400
Today, half a million acres of this arid land have become the Atomic Energy Commission's

11
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:24,620
National Reactor Testing Station, commonly referred to as NRTS.

12
00:01:24,620 --> 00:01:29,360
Part of this tract was once a naval proving ground, where some of the biggest bullets

13
00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:35,540
ever made, naval artillery holes, were tested against massive blocks of concrete.

14
00:01:35,540 --> 00:01:41,719
Today, we deal here with some of the smallest bullets in the universe, with which atoms

15
00:01:41,719 --> 00:01:44,760
are smashed to release atomic energy.

16
00:01:44,760 --> 00:01:51,079
And releasing atomic, or more precisely, nuclear energy, is the prime business of this section

17
00:01:51,079 --> 00:01:54,280
of southeastern Idaho.

18
00:01:54,280 --> 00:01:59,200
Here there are more reactors of more advanced and different types than in any equivalent

19
00:01:59,200 --> 00:02:00,340
anywhere.

20
00:02:00,340 --> 00:02:06,200
This rolling desert plain has been nicknamed prototype prairie because here the experimental

21
00:02:06,200 --> 00:02:11,840
First Models, or prototypes, of power-producing nuclear reactors are built and put through

22
00:02:11,840 --> 00:02:13,740
their paces.

23
00:02:13,740 --> 00:02:20,379
Our greatest test reactors are also located here, reactors in whose fierce atomic fires

24
00:02:20,379 --> 00:02:27,580
we test the very materials out of which we hope to build new and better reactors.

25
00:02:27,580 --> 00:02:32,319
Locate on a map the hundreds of reactors built, planned, or under construction in this country

26
00:02:32,319 --> 00:02:34,680
alone in recent years.

27
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:38,780
Almost every one of them owes a heavy debt to the work at NRTS.

28
00:02:38,780 --> 00:02:46,040
Indeed, many could not have been built at all until that work was done.

29
00:02:46,040 --> 00:02:51,120
Because most of our knowledge is freely shared, these threads of obligation to the testing

30
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:57,560
station also reach around the world to numerous foreign countries where new reactors are profiting

31
00:02:57,560 --> 00:02:59,740
from our experience.

32
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:06,080
The reactor testing station has deep importance, not only to builders of reactors, but to countless

33
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:11,719
millions of people who never heard of the place, who may never even see a reactor, let

34
00:03:11,719 --> 00:03:14,260
alone build one.

35
00:03:14,260 --> 00:03:20,379
This importance stems from a shortage that is not present today, but may be closer than

36
00:03:20,379 --> 00:03:27,980
is generally realized, a shortage of energy, of power.

37
00:03:27,980 --> 00:03:33,100
Almost every tangible aspect of our civilization is based on a tremendous consumption of cheap

38
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:40,740
power chiefly supplied by the burning of our fossil fuels, coal, natural gas and petroleum.

39
00:03:40,740 --> 00:03:45,980
We are now finding out that our world simply does not contain enough of these fossil fuels

40
00:03:45,980 --> 00:03:50,900
to allow us to go on spending them at our constantly increasing rate for any great time

41
00:03:50,900 --> 00:03:53,080
in the future.

42
00:03:53,080 --> 00:03:57,960
This bleak prospect has been altered by the advent of nuclear energy, which is already

43
00:03:57,960 --> 00:04:03,360
helping meet the world's sharply rising demands for power.

44
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:08,560
Also nuclear power plants generate their energy without adding to air pollution.

45
00:04:08,560 --> 00:04:13,960
But more important, they conserve more of our irreplaceable fossil fuels for making

46
00:04:13,960 --> 00:04:19,400
steel, plastics, medicines, gasoline.

47
00:04:19,399 --> 00:04:24,139
These then, are some of the compelling reasons why so many experimental reactors of so many

48
00:04:24,139 --> 00:04:28,359
different types are being built in this Idaho testing area.

49
00:04:28,359 --> 00:04:34,239
Our nation's hopes for the development of tomorrow's power are centered in these reactors,

50
00:04:34,239 --> 00:04:40,620
silent furnaces that burn a cold, shiny metal, uranium.

51
00:04:40,620 --> 00:04:47,079
Today most people find no strangeness in our concept that all matter is made up of atoms.

52
00:04:47,079 --> 00:04:58,060
Natural uranium is a mixture of two kinds of metallic atoms, uranium-238 and uranium-235.

53
00:04:58,060 --> 00:05:04,699
Most of these atoms are relatively stable uranium-238, or more simply, U-238.

54
00:05:04,699 --> 00:05:12,519
Only one of every 140 natural uranium atoms is the richer, more excitable U-235.

55
00:05:12,519 --> 00:05:15,180
Both metals look exactly the same.

56
00:05:15,180 --> 00:05:20,480
fact, they can't be separated chemically. Now if one of the tiny speeding particles

57
00:05:20,480 --> 00:05:26,639
that we call neutrons happens to collide with an atom of U-238, it may simply

58
00:05:26,639 --> 00:05:33,360
bounce off, or it may be captured and thus absorbed into the atom. If, on the

59
00:05:33,360 --> 00:05:39,840
other hand, the neutron happens to hit a U-235 atom, it may split or fission the

60
00:05:39,840 --> 00:05:46,080
atom into several fragments. Part of the fissioning atom changes from matter into

61
00:05:46,080 --> 00:05:52,860
energy, most of it appearing as heat and penetrating radiation. In this release of

62
00:05:52,860 --> 00:05:59,840
energy, atomic power is born. The leftover pieces of the broken atom are called

63
00:05:59,840 --> 00:06:05,760
fission products. They are intensely radioactive and are now entirely

64
00:06:05,759 --> 00:06:13,259
different elements of lighter weight, no longer uranium of any kind. A very important part of

65
00:06:13,259 --> 00:06:18,839
the fission process is that the breaking atom throws out two or three of its own neutrons,

66
00:06:18,839 --> 00:06:27,199
each one of which may in turn hit and fission still another atom of U-235. Thus we may obtain

67
00:06:27,199 --> 00:06:33,879
a constantly increasing number of fissions, a chain reaction, the reactor people call it. And

68
00:06:33,879 --> 00:06:37,980
And that brings us to reactors.

69
00:06:37,980 --> 00:06:44,879
In a reactor, we try to keep a chain reaction going at an even rather than increasing rate

70
00:06:44,879 --> 00:06:49,339
to give us a steady and controlled release of power.

71
00:06:49,339 --> 00:06:53,980
We can illustrate the basic reactor concept by starting with a simplified version of a

72
00:06:53,980 --> 00:06:55,899
reactor core.

73
00:06:55,899 --> 00:07:02,199
This may consist of a number of plates or rods of uranium metal called fuel elements,

74
00:07:02,199 --> 00:07:07,479
enriched by addition of an extra percentage of the fissionable U-235, both

75
00:07:07,479 --> 00:07:12,839
for compactness and so that it will be easier to start and maintain a chain

76
00:07:12,839 --> 00:07:20,519
reaction. The core will be located inside of a tank or reactor vessel and outside

77
00:07:20,519 --> 00:07:25,180
of it we will want a heavy shield of metal or concrete to protect working

78
00:07:25,180 --> 00:07:31,579
personnel from radiation. Next, we will fill up the spaces between the uranium

79
00:07:31,579 --> 00:07:38,779
plates with water graphite paraffin or any other material that will do a job that we call

80
00:07:38,779 --> 00:07:46,459
moderating this moderator slows down the swiftly traveling neutrons to speeds at which they have

81
00:07:46,459 --> 00:07:53,819
the best chance of causing new fissions rather than being captured by the u-238 atoms or bouncing

82
00:07:53,819 --> 00:08:01,099
off and finally escaping entirely we selected water for the moderator in this simplified example

83
00:08:01,100 --> 00:08:04,900
because it also does such a good job as a coolant,

84
00:08:04,900 --> 00:08:09,900
transferring or carrying away the heat of the fissioning atoms.

85
00:08:09,900 --> 00:08:14,200
Now, with the water in place and the fuel elements correctly positioned,

86
00:08:14,200 --> 00:08:16,100
the reactor is ready.

87
00:08:16,100 --> 00:08:21,200
In fact, it would produce energy much faster than we wish, except for one thing.

88
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:27,200
We also insert in the core a number of control rods.

89
00:08:27,199 --> 00:08:38,200
These are normally filled with some material like cadmium or boron that will quickly absorb many of the increasing numbers of neutrons and subdue the fission chain reaction.

90
00:08:38,200 --> 00:08:49,200
As soon as we begin to pull the control rods out of the core, the rate of fissioning will again increase up to the level determined by the given rod adjustment.

91
00:08:49,200 --> 00:08:53,200
When the control rods hold the fission rate at just the right level,

92
00:08:53,200 --> 00:08:58,200
the reactor is critical with the controlled and sustained chain reaction.

93
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:05,200
The steady release of heat makes the moderating water boil and give off steam.

94
00:09:05,200 --> 00:09:11,200
If we now cap the top of our reactor tank, we have a working steam boiler.

95
00:09:11,200 --> 00:09:16,200
We refer to this type of arrangement as a boiling water reactor.

96
00:09:16,200 --> 00:09:21,280
We can now pipe the steam off to drive a steam turbine and electrical generator,

97
00:09:21,580 --> 00:09:24,620
and we have a full-fledged electric power plant.

98
00:09:28,460 --> 00:09:33,300
A variation on this design is called a pressurized water reactor.

99
00:09:34,180 --> 00:09:37,400
In this, the cooling and moderating water is kept under high pressure

100
00:09:37,400 --> 00:09:42,320
so that it will not boil and turn into steam, even though it gets extremely hot.

101
00:09:42,320 --> 00:09:49,760
circulating through a closed circuit this water passes through a heat exchanger which transfers

102
00:09:49,760 --> 00:09:56,280
heat to a completely separate second flow of water inside this second stage the heat

103
00:09:56,280 --> 00:10:04,340
does produce steam to drive a turbine and generator in some reactors the moderator

104
00:10:04,340 --> 00:10:10,880
may be a solid like graphite and the coolant may be a substance other than water sometimes

105
00:10:10,879 --> 00:10:16,039
Sometimes gas or even molten metal are circulated through the core to carry the heat to the

106
00:10:16,039 --> 00:10:17,860
heat exchanger.

107
00:10:17,860 --> 00:10:25,039
A great many variations are possible, but almost all power reactors in Idaho or anywhere

108
00:10:25,039 --> 00:10:29,080
else use basically the same simple principles.

109
00:10:29,080 --> 00:10:34,740
However, the theoretical and engineering jobs that translate these principles into useful

110
00:10:34,740 --> 00:10:39,159
power are tremendously complex.

111
00:10:39,159 --> 00:10:43,399
Look for instance the uranium fuel elements of the reactors.

112
00:10:43,399 --> 00:10:50,039
Perhaps the fuel is used in the form of slim, plated, perfectly machined and polished rods.

113
00:10:50,039 --> 00:10:54,939
Perhaps the fuel is sandwiched between sheets of protective metal called cladding, then

114
00:10:54,939 --> 00:11:01,759
rolled out thin and finally cut and welded into a precisely dimensioned rectangular grid

115
00:11:01,759 --> 00:11:05,559
or some other machinist's nightmare.

116
00:11:05,560 --> 00:11:09,600
How enriched should the uranium be for best results?

117
00:11:09,600 --> 00:11:15,400
Shall the protective cladding be of aluminum, zirconium, stainless steel, or what?

118
00:11:15,400 --> 00:11:21,380
Sometimes the fuel does not consist of metallic uranium at all, but of a ceramic form, such

119
00:11:21,380 --> 00:11:29,480
as uranium oxide, again, possibly in metal-clad plates, rods, or even small pellets.

120
00:11:29,480 --> 00:11:30,680
And so on.

121
00:11:30,680 --> 00:11:34,200
It is possible to ring scores of changes on this solid fuel

122
00:11:34,200 --> 00:11:40,280
question alone, but every good idea must be tested before being used.

123
00:11:40,280 --> 00:11:44,920
Similarly, the coolants must be tested, the moderators,

124
00:11:44,920 --> 00:11:50,180
the heat exchange methods and materials, and 1,001 other items.

125
00:11:50,180 --> 00:11:53,180
First as experiments in test reactors,

126
00:11:53,180 --> 00:11:58,160
and later as part of reactor prototypes themselves.

127
00:11:58,159 --> 00:12:01,419
These are the jobs that get done at NRTS,

128
00:12:01,419 --> 00:12:06,919
where the proof of the pudding is in the successful functioning of the reactors.

129
00:12:06,919 --> 00:12:10,719
But the NRTS is not all reactors.

130
00:12:10,719 --> 00:12:16,779
Over in the central facilities area of the station, the Atomic Energy Commission maintains a number of services

131
00:12:16,779 --> 00:12:21,379
in support of the various nuclear facilities.

132
00:12:21,379 --> 00:12:24,399
Many of these facilities and all of the reactors

133
00:12:24,399 --> 00:12:28,519
are operated for the government by private contractors.

134
00:12:28,519 --> 00:12:33,959
These contractors include some important names in American industry, working here in partnership

135
00:12:33,959 --> 00:12:39,220
with the American government to learn the secrets of tomorrow's power at this unique

136
00:12:39,220 --> 00:12:43,559
engineering laboratory in the desert.

137
00:12:43,559 --> 00:12:48,860
The isolation out here gives us a chance for some large savings in developmental time and

138
00:12:48,860 --> 00:12:57,779
expense, since it permits us to do extremely valuable destructive testing.

139
00:12:57,779 --> 00:13:05,019
An early case in point was BORAX-1, the first of our experimental boiling water reactors.

140
00:13:05,019 --> 00:13:09,659
Tests to demonstrate the basic stability of this type of reactor were deliberately carried

141
00:13:09,659 --> 00:13:16,539
to extreme limits until the power surges produced experimentally became so large that they destroyed

142
00:13:16,539 --> 00:13:18,759
the reactor.

143
00:13:18,759 --> 00:13:23,639
We learned from these tests that such a blowup could be made almost impossible in normal

144
00:13:23,639 --> 00:13:25,439
operation.

145
00:13:25,439 --> 00:13:30,100
Still we would not have dared to conduct such deliberate terminal testing in a populated

146
00:13:30,100 --> 00:13:32,899
area.

147
00:13:32,899 --> 00:13:37,700
The findings of the first borax experiments were of great value in the design and testing

148
00:13:37,700 --> 00:13:44,600
of later reactors in the same family, lineal descendants, which build on and vastly extend

149
00:13:44,600 --> 00:13:46,919
the earlier knowledge.

150
00:13:46,920 --> 00:13:52,960
In the genealogy of experimental borax reactors, number three was unique in being the first

151
00:13:52,960 --> 00:13:58,520
to provide sufficient electricity from nuclear heat to illuminate the streets and homes of

152
00:13:58,520 --> 00:14:03,380
an entire American community.

153
00:14:03,380 --> 00:14:08,920
Borax four, in the same building, gave us valuable new data on conversion of thorium

154
00:14:08,920 --> 00:14:13,400
to fissionable uranium.

155
00:14:13,399 --> 00:14:20,720
A little later, Borax-5 was designed to investigate the production of superheated steam by recirculating

156
00:14:20,720 --> 00:14:25,720
the moist steam produced by one set of fuel elements in the core through a second set

157
00:14:25,720 --> 00:14:29,019
of elements in the same core.

158
00:14:29,019 --> 00:14:35,759
The resulting drier, hotter steam, almost 900 degrees Fahrenheit, is comparable to temperatures

159
00:14:35,759 --> 00:14:40,259
produced in the most efficient conventional power plants.

160
00:14:40,259 --> 00:14:47,039
The Borax-5 experiments have been important steps toward lower cost power from the atom.

161
00:14:47,039 --> 00:14:52,480
An important outgrowth of the early borax experiments is another family line of reactors

162
00:14:52,480 --> 00:14:58,220
known as SPERT or special power excursion reactor tests.

163
00:14:58,220 --> 00:15:03,439
A major portion of the commission's intensive program of reactor safety investigations is

164
00:15:03,439 --> 00:15:05,460
carried out here.

165
00:15:05,460 --> 00:15:10,320
The aim is to develop knowledge of reactor behavior so that nuclear power plants can

166
00:15:10,320 --> 00:15:17,000
be designed for greater safety, operational flexibility, and less cost.

167
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:22,540
By pushing the SPERT reactors to extreme limits, their ultimate self-shutdown mechanisms can

168
00:15:22,540 --> 00:15:30,680
be studied under a wide range of conditions and with many different cores.

169
00:15:30,680 --> 00:15:36,920
the open vessel Spert 1 has been deliberately pushed to extreme limits, and on November

170
00:15:36,920 --> 00:15:45,140
5th, 1962, a highly enriched plate-type core was completely destroyed.

171
00:15:45,140 --> 00:15:51,720
Yet in subsequent tests, a low-enriched uranium oxide core successfully withstood even greater

172
00:15:51,720 --> 00:15:57,540
power bursts, a development of major safety significance to the general public because

173
00:15:57,539 --> 00:16:04,500
The same kind of fuel is used in large nuclear power stations.

174
00:16:04,500 --> 00:16:10,699
Spurt 2 can be operated as a closed and pressurized system at raised temperatures and using a

175
00:16:10,699 --> 00:16:18,919
variety of moderators including heavy water.

176
00:16:18,919 --> 00:16:24,099
Spurt 3 is considered the most versatile facility yet developed for studying high power reactor

177
00:16:24,099 --> 00:16:25,539
safety.

178
00:16:25,540 --> 00:16:31,900
It provides for operation at high power levels and with different cores with slow or very

179
00:16:31,900 --> 00:16:38,520
fast coolant flows, water temperatures up to 650 degrees Fahrenheit, and pressures up

180
00:16:38,520 --> 00:16:44,120
to 2,500 pounds per square inch.

181
00:16:44,120 --> 00:16:47,360
The youngster of the SPERT line is number four.

182
00:16:47,360 --> 00:16:53,260
This is another open pool reactor with greatly improved facilities for exploring power fluctuation

183
00:16:53,259 --> 00:16:58,899
problems beyond the capabilities of SPERT 1.

184
00:16:58,899 --> 00:17:04,779
In addition to its larger coolant reservoir and resultant heat absorption capacity, larger

185
00:17:04,779 --> 00:17:09,619
cores can be examined in SPERT 4.

186
00:17:09,619 --> 00:17:15,839
It should be realized that the knowledge generated at SPERT, as well as other NRTS facilities,

187
00:17:15,839 --> 00:17:25,379
has almost simultaneous impact on the outside world of both American and foreign industry.

188
00:17:25,379 --> 00:17:31,639
Data from our earliest NRTS studies have influenced the design of our most advanced boiling water

189
00:17:31,639 --> 00:17:33,119
reactors.

190
00:17:33,119 --> 00:17:38,419
Even as first generation developments, these plants made significant contributions to lower

191
00:17:38,419 --> 00:17:43,019
cost electricity from atomic heat.

192
00:17:43,019 --> 00:17:48,579
The concept of the pressurized water reactor as distinct from the boiling type has proceeded

193
00:17:48,579 --> 00:17:53,440
along two different routes, military and civilian.

194
00:17:53,440 --> 00:17:58,259
A notable achievement in the civilian direction is the Shipping Port Atomic Power Station

195
00:17:58,259 --> 00:18:04,259
in Pennsylvania, the nation's first large-scale nuclear plant to generate electricity for

196
00:18:04,259 --> 00:18:06,519
civilian power grids.

197
00:18:06,519 --> 00:18:12,619
However, the important bulk of the Idaho prototype testing for reactors of this pressurized water

198
00:18:12,620 --> 00:18:19,100
category was military in its first application aimed at freeing our naval vessels from their

199
00:18:19,100 --> 00:18:25,020
ancient need for either frequent returns to port for refueling or the alternative of potentially

200
00:18:25,020 --> 00:18:29,620
hazardous refueling at sea.

201
00:18:29,620 --> 00:18:34,440
In particular, submarines have always been handicapped by refueling needs and by the

202
00:18:34,440 --> 00:18:39,860
much more dangerous requirement of frequent surfacing to recharge batteries even in active

203
00:18:39,860 --> 00:18:46,520
battle zones the possibilities of a submarine with atomic engines to free

204
00:18:46,520 --> 00:18:50,420
itself from the Earth's atmosphere thus giving it almost limitless range

205
00:18:50,420 --> 00:18:57,360
underwater offered the promise of revolutionizing naval warfare picked

206
00:18:57,360 --> 00:19:02,060
construction crews built a full-scale submarine inside a well-guarded building

207
00:19:02,060 --> 00:19:08,380
at NRTS and installed a full-scale nuclear engine the remarkable

208
00:19:08,380 --> 00:19:13,580
performance of this ship in the desert resulted in the world's first nuclear-powered ship,

209
00:19:13,580 --> 00:19:22,460
the famed submarine Nautilus, and the subsequent addition to the U.S. fleet of numerous atomic

210
00:19:22,460 --> 00:19:32,560
sister ships, many of which carry the mighty punch of Polaris missiles. Pressurized water

211
00:19:32,560 --> 00:19:38,180
reactors were also selected for the next big job of the station's naval facility, development

212
00:19:38,180 --> 00:19:43,779
of the land-based prototype of the engines which now power large naval surface ships,

213
00:19:43,779 --> 00:19:49,340
such as the missile cruiser Long Beach and the giant aircraft carrier Enterprise, which

214
00:19:49,340 --> 00:19:55,200
actually have to hold down their effortless speed to avoid running away from their non-nuclear

215
00:19:55,200 --> 00:19:57,960
escorting destroyers.

216
00:19:57,960 --> 00:20:03,580
A more recent prototype reactor under development in the Naval Reactors Area at NRTS is the

217
00:20:03,579 --> 00:20:10,379
S5G, the natural circulation reactor, which uses no pumps but only natural convection

218
00:20:10,379 --> 00:20:13,379
to circulate its cooling water.

219
00:20:13,379 --> 00:20:17,939
Many of our military operations must be carried out in remote areas which can be supplied

220
00:20:17,939 --> 00:20:23,099
with conventional fuel for heat and electric power only through great effort and expense

221
00:20:23,099 --> 00:20:26,119
and sometimes at substantial risk.

222
00:20:26,119 --> 00:20:32,359
NRTS has been the home of a major part of the Army's mobile reactor development program,

223
00:20:32,360 --> 00:20:37,500
which of course is also of great interest to the other services for electric power production.

224
00:20:37,500 --> 00:20:44,240
A first stage in the approach to nuclear power plants that can be moved without disassembly

225
00:20:44,240 --> 00:20:49,460
was the GCRE, gas-cooled reactor experiment.

226
00:20:49,460 --> 00:20:57,540
GCRE's major claim to fame is that it was the proud parent of ML1, the Army's mobile

227
00:20:57,540 --> 00:21:00,920
low-power reactor plant number one.

228
00:21:00,920 --> 00:21:07,320
This is the world's first direct and closed cycle gas-cooled nuclear power system and

229
00:21:07,320 --> 00:21:11,380
the first prototype of a truly mobile power reactor.

230
00:21:11,380 --> 00:21:16,320
The reactor and the turbo generator systems weigh about 15 tons each and are mounted on

231
00:21:16,320 --> 00:21:18,160
separate skids.

232
00:21:18,160 --> 00:21:25,420
Both can be transported in a single airplane or by truck trailer, barge, ship, or sled.

233
00:21:25,420 --> 00:21:30,759
The much lighter transistorized control cab, also skid mounted, can be carried on a two

234
00:21:30,759 --> 00:21:32,900
and a half ton truck.

235
00:21:32,900 --> 00:21:38,940
The entire plant is designed to be moved 24 hours after shut down and be operating again

236
00:21:38,940 --> 00:21:45,620
within 12 hours after arrival at a new location, turning out 300 to 500 kilowatts of electric

237
00:21:45,620 --> 00:21:53,519
power for almost two years on a single loading of nuclear fuel.

238
00:21:53,519 --> 00:21:59,139
A few miles farther west is another variation on the nuclear power plant theme, the Organic

239
00:21:59,139 --> 00:22:04,619
Moderated Reactor Experiment, now retired from active duty.

240
00:22:04,619 --> 00:22:10,759
OMRE's experimental work is now completed, but some of its family traits have been inherited

241
00:22:10,759 --> 00:22:19,119
by organic-cooled reactors at several sites in Europe, Canada, and in the United States.

242
00:22:19,120 --> 00:22:24,160
Another important family of reactors at the station is found in the test reactors area.

243
00:22:24,160 --> 00:22:29,400
The primary purpose here is to find out in weeks or months what would otherwise take

244
00:22:29,400 --> 00:22:33,860
years to discover in normal reactor operation.

245
00:22:33,860 --> 00:22:41,080
The oldest, in a sense, the granddaddy of the family, is the MTR, the Materials Testing

246
00:22:41,080 --> 00:22:45,980
Reactor, a name that has become famous in scientific circles during its tens of thousands

247
00:22:45,980 --> 00:22:48,200
of operating hours.

248
00:22:48,200 --> 00:22:54,840
reactor built or being built in the world today is directly or indirectly indebted to the MTR.

249
00:22:56,120 --> 00:23:02,360
Through or to regions closely surrounding the core of this water-cooled reactor go two kinds

250
00:23:02,360 --> 00:23:09,240
of pipes. These loops and irradiation holes make it possible to insert sample substances for

251
00:23:09,240 --> 00:23:14,840
exposure to intense gamma rays and a tremendous flux of more than a hundred million million

252
00:23:14,839 --> 00:23:20,179
bombarding neutrons per second over every square inch.

253
00:23:20,179 --> 00:23:25,399
These tests are extremely important because of the sometimes weird and unexpected things

254
00:23:25,399 --> 00:23:31,319
that heat and nuclear radiation can do to reactor construction materials.

255
00:23:31,319 --> 00:23:36,639
Most particularly, we must know how fuel materials and their alloys behave.

256
00:23:36,639 --> 00:23:42,500
Some samples bow or twist or bulge in ways that might lead to cladding ruptures and escapes

257
00:23:42,500 --> 00:23:49,099
of radioactivity. Such things must be known before the materials go into our military

258
00:23:49,099 --> 00:23:56,980
and civilian nuclear plants, or we risk dangerous and extremely expensive mistakes.

259
00:23:56,980 --> 00:24:02,339
The MTR's primary role of testing has been augmented by extensive research and development

260
00:24:02,339 --> 00:24:09,519
work in neutron physics. For such purposes, as illustrated in this top view, we use beam

261
00:24:09,519 --> 00:24:14,379
holes through the heavy shielding to tap the reactor's reservoir of neutrons and

262
00:24:14,379 --> 00:24:19,779
channel them through a variety of measuring devices. One measuring device

263
00:24:19,779 --> 00:24:25,960
is the neutron crystal spectrometer. It can bend and in this way select out of a

264
00:24:25,960 --> 00:24:31,480
wide range neutrons of any given speed that we want to work with. Two other

265
00:24:31,480 --> 00:24:36,440
instruments working at beam holes use very fast rotating shutters and time of

266
00:24:36,440 --> 00:24:41,240
flight methods to sort out and measure the different neutron energies during millionth

267
00:24:41,240 --> 00:24:43,779
of a second pulses.

268
00:24:43,779 --> 00:24:49,580
Comparing the number of neutrons before and after their beam bombards a sample material

269
00:24:49,580 --> 00:24:54,519
tells us how hungry that material is for neutrons of each energy.

270
00:24:54,519 --> 00:25:00,140
Knowing the neutron appetites enables reactor designers to predict how desirable this or

271
00:25:00,140 --> 00:25:04,980
that material will be as a reactor component.

272
00:25:04,980 --> 00:25:11,140
The MTR is also world renowned for its production of custom-tailored radioisotopes.

273
00:25:11,140 --> 00:25:17,059
Common elements, ordinarily not radioactive, are changed and given remarkable properties

274
00:25:17,059 --> 00:25:21,059
by exposure to the MTR's high neutron flux.

275
00:25:21,059 --> 00:25:28,400
For example, cobalt-59 becomes radioactive cobalt-60, hundreds of times more potent than

276
00:25:28,400 --> 00:25:31,799
radium for cancer therapy.

277
00:25:31,799 --> 00:25:38,279
Special orders from all over the world are filled, such as irradiation of an iron bolt

278
00:25:38,279 --> 00:25:47,839
for a laboratory in Japan or high specific activity calcium for a Swedish hospital.

279
00:25:47,839 --> 00:25:53,700
Nearby is an integral part of the test reactor program called the Advanced Reactivity Measurement

280
00:25:53,700 --> 00:25:54,700
Facility.

281
00:25:54,700 --> 00:26:01,000
Here, two low power reactors measure changes in the nuclear properties of material samples

282
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:07,380
before and after irradiation, a chore they perform with unequaled sensitivity.

283
00:26:07,380 --> 00:26:12,400
The ARMF is another vitally needed approach to improve reactor design.

284
00:26:12,400 --> 00:26:16,779
It can tell us in a matter of minutes and with only a tiny sample

285
00:26:16,779 --> 00:26:20,339
whether millions of dollars' worth of a potential atomic fuel

286
00:26:20,339 --> 00:26:27,240
will do the job expected, obviously a huge economy.

287
00:26:27,240 --> 00:26:34,480
South of the MTR is the Engineering Test Reactor, or ETR, which grew out of the overwhelming

288
00:26:34,480 --> 00:26:37,099
demands on its predecessor.

289
00:26:37,099 --> 00:26:44,559
This newer giant, with 175 million watts of heat output to the MTR's 40 million, and with

290
00:26:44,559 --> 00:26:50,539
loops that go right through the core, can handle much bigger samples than the MTR, and

291
00:26:50,539 --> 00:26:56,759
at higher temperatures, higher pressures, and with a much higher neutron flux.

292
00:26:56,759 --> 00:27:03,000
The northwest of the MTR is the newest, most advanced, and largest of the three major test reactors,

293
00:27:03,759 --> 00:27:08,440
appropriately named ATR for Advanced Test Reactor.

294
00:27:10,500 --> 00:27:15,319
Construction of the ATR was started in late 1961 and held to a tight schedule

295
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:22,640
because of the urgent need for accumulating data more rapidly to keep pace with fast-moving reactor technology.

296
00:27:22,640 --> 00:27:30,800
The ATR has a heat energy output of 250 million watts, nearly half again as much as that of the

297
00:27:30,800 --> 00:27:38,240
ETR. Its ingenious core design permits multiple yet simultaneous irradiations under a wide choice

298
00:27:38,240 --> 00:27:47,120
of conditions. The ATR core contains nine loop spaces which can take long vertical pipes as

299
00:27:47,120 --> 00:27:49,240
as large as five inches in diameter.

300
00:27:50,420 --> 00:27:54,420
These can hold solid specimens up to four feet long,

301
00:27:54,420 --> 00:27:57,880
and each pipe can be made a completely closed test loop

302
00:27:57,880 --> 00:28:00,960
with its own pumps, heaters, or what have you

303
00:28:00,960 --> 00:28:04,600
to circulate liquids, gases, or slurries,

304
00:28:04,600 --> 00:28:07,580
pressurized and hot, or the reverse.

305
00:28:08,640 --> 00:28:13,500
Looking down at the nine pipes, we see the truly unique feature of ATR,

306
00:28:13,500 --> 00:28:16,120
the cloverleaf arrangement of the fuel elements.

307
00:28:17,120 --> 00:28:25,120
The power levels in different regions of this serpentine core can be adjusted to provide different neutron fluxes for individual loops,

308
00:28:25,160 --> 00:28:31,880
a time and money-saving flexibility that could otherwise be accomplished only by nine separate reactors

309
00:28:31,880 --> 00:28:39,460
operating at a combined power level three times greater and therefore much costlier than the ATR.

310
00:28:40,560 --> 00:28:46,680
The MTR, ETR, and ATR are huge and expensive machines.

311
00:28:47,120 --> 00:28:54,260
But without them, reactor design would still be limping blindly in its earlier phases.

312
00:28:54,260 --> 00:28:59,380
No one today can foresee a time when these remarkable tools will stop paying again and

313
00:28:59,380 --> 00:29:04,020
again for their original cost.

314
00:29:04,020 --> 00:29:08,480
Strangely enough at first thought, a much smaller test unit in another area can produce

315
00:29:08,480 --> 00:29:14,260
a substantially greater neutron flux than any of the three giant test but only for very

316
00:29:14,260 --> 00:29:15,760
short intervals.

317
00:29:15,759 --> 00:29:22,079
However, the rate at which the flux increases is so rapid that very important tests can

318
00:29:22,079 --> 00:29:28,700
be performed in the transient reactor test facility, or TREAT, as it's commonly called.

319
00:29:28,700 --> 00:29:34,480
By limiting its high power output to very brief transient pulses, this reactor fuel

320
00:29:34,480 --> 00:29:40,400
and safety test facility can safely flood its experimental spaces with enormous concentrations

321
00:29:40,400 --> 00:29:42,460
of neutrons.

322
00:29:42,460 --> 00:29:49,620
materials in the spaces can thus be subjected to fluxes and heats far higher and more destructive

323
00:29:49,620 --> 00:29:53,799
than they are ever expected to receive in actual service.

324
00:29:53,799 --> 00:29:59,700
And the way in which test fuel elements, for example, fail, if they do fail, tells us how

325
00:29:59,700 --> 00:30:03,059
we can improve reactor design.

326
00:30:03,059 --> 00:30:07,980
High-speed cameras look inside the reactor to record results when the neutron barrage

327
00:30:07,980 --> 00:30:13,640
causes a sample fuel element to react so violently that its cladding ruptures.

328
00:30:13,640 --> 00:30:18,799
Vaporized sodium bonding material and molten uranium fuel SPERT out of the

329
00:30:18,799 --> 00:30:22,620
open seam when failure occurs.

330
00:30:26,360 --> 00:30:33,720
Twenty miles north is TAN, a complex called Test Area North. This was

331
00:30:33,720 --> 00:30:38,860
originally the site of ANP, where significant progress toward development of a nuclear

332
00:30:38,860 --> 00:30:41,880
airplane was made in the 1950s.

333
00:30:41,880 --> 00:30:45,400
Since that project was discontinued in 1961,

334
00:30:45,400 --> 00:30:49,059
several other programs have been assigned to the area.

335
00:30:49,059 --> 00:30:54,500
The hub of activities in this area is the Technical Services Facility, or TSF.

336
00:30:54,500 --> 00:30:56,940
Here are several large shops.

337
00:30:56,940 --> 00:31:02,420
The coal shop handles hardware assembly jobs involving no radioactivity.

338
00:31:02,420 --> 00:31:09,920
The warm shop receives slightly radioactive assemblies for servicing, adjustment or modification.

339
00:31:09,920 --> 00:31:15,700
And for intensely radioactive materials, there is a mammoth hot shop equipped with remote

340
00:31:15,700 --> 00:31:18,539
handling devices of several types.

341
00:31:18,539 --> 00:31:25,360
Some for delicate and precise work and others massive enough for industrial-sized operations.

342
00:31:25,360 --> 00:31:30,400
The service shops and other existing facilities in which testing is carried out are being

343
00:31:30,400 --> 00:31:36,300
shared to great advantage by new programs assigned to Test Area North.

344
00:31:36,300 --> 00:31:41,840
The testing facilities are situated in a fan-shaped pattern around the services area.

345
00:31:41,840 --> 00:31:46,000
Best known is the initial engineering test facility.

346
00:31:46,000 --> 00:31:52,380
A four-track railroad connects it with the service shops, thus permitting reactor experiments

347
00:31:52,380 --> 00:31:59,380
to be shuttled back and forth on double-width flat cars for testing and post-test analysis.

348
00:31:59,380 --> 00:32:06,580
This unique feature has made the IET a valuable adjunct to safety testing.

349
00:32:06,580 --> 00:32:19,440
A case in point was the testing of a SNAP-10A auxiliary power space reactor in April 1964.

350
00:32:19,440 --> 00:32:24,200
The results showed that such a device would immediately destroy itself instead of building

351
00:32:24,200 --> 00:32:31,960
up a high inventory of radioactive fission products should it fall into water.

352
00:32:31,960 --> 00:32:38,220
Tests like this are part of STEP, the Safety Test Engineering Program, which involves engineering

353
00:32:38,220 --> 00:32:46,460
scale field testing to demonstrate the safety of aerospace and land-based reactor systems.

354
00:32:46,460 --> 00:32:52,580
Another STEP testing complex two miles to the west is the Field Engineering Test Facility.

355
00:32:52,580 --> 00:32:58,080
for operation here is the loss of fluid test called loft which is designed to

356
00:32:58,080 --> 00:33:01,280
investigate the worst accident that conceivably could happen to a

357
00:33:01,280 --> 00:33:09,860
water-cooled reactor. Also in test area north is the 710 research reactor

358
00:33:09,860 --> 00:33:14,700
experiment studying the use in reactor design of refractory metals such as

359
00:33:14,700 --> 00:33:19,740
tungsten and tantalum. These metals can maintain high strength even when

360
00:33:19,740 --> 00:33:25,480
extremely hot but are metallurgically very difficult to fabricate. Hence, a large study

361
00:33:25,480 --> 00:33:31,240
effort is required to make use of their virtues.

362
00:33:31,240 --> 00:33:38,579
Next door to 710 is EBOR, the experimental beryllium oxide reactor facility. This helium

363
00:33:38,579 --> 00:33:45,680
gas cooled experiment is a promising concept for low cost electricity and process heat.

364
00:33:45,680 --> 00:33:52,440
Near the center of the NRTS is a support facility of importance to the entire station.

365
00:33:52,440 --> 00:33:59,640
This is the ICPP, or chem plant, the site's biggest single plant investment and a final

366
00:33:59,640 --> 00:34:05,120
destination of burned out or expended fuel elements removed from reactors throughout

367
00:34:05,120 --> 00:34:07,880
the free world.

368
00:34:07,880 --> 00:34:12,200
These spent elements are not burned out in the sense that a stick of wood burns out in

369
00:34:12,199 --> 00:34:19,739
of fire. There is no complete utilization of the fuel. Even after a reactor core has

370
00:34:19,739 --> 00:34:25,819
operated for hundreds of hours, only a matter of ounces of uranium will have been fissioned,

371
00:34:25,819 --> 00:34:30,359
perhaps less than a quarter of the load. By then, however, the fragments of shattered

372
00:34:30,359 --> 00:34:36,319
atoms that we call fission products will be soaking up so many of the bombarding neutrons

373
00:34:36,320 --> 00:34:39,460
that a chain reaction cannot continue.

374
00:34:39,460 --> 00:34:41,960
The core is effectively poisoned.

375
00:34:43,600 --> 00:34:46,760
Then the fuel must go into the chem plant for processing

376
00:34:46,760 --> 00:34:50,320
to dispose of the poisons and salvage the unused uranium

377
00:34:50,320 --> 00:34:51,940
for new fuel elements.

378
00:34:53,039 --> 00:34:57,580
Through an engineering triumph called the continuous dissolution process,

379
00:34:57,580 --> 00:35:04,500
spent elements can now be fed into the plant stream as fast as desired without interrupting the processing cycle.

380
00:35:04,500 --> 00:35:12,500
99.9 percent of the precious unburned U-235, worth $300 an ounce,

381
00:35:12,500 --> 00:35:15,500
is recovered for reuse.

382
00:35:15,500 --> 00:35:19,500
Samples of the plant stream can be drawn off at various stages

383
00:35:19,500 --> 00:35:23,500
for analysis in thick-windowed hot cells.

384
00:35:23,500 --> 00:35:28,500
Still, other cells have facilities for separation of very valuable radioactive elements,

385
00:35:28,500 --> 00:35:32,500
such as iodine-131 and cesium-137

386
00:35:32,500 --> 00:35:36,059
from the fission product soup of the mainstream.

387
00:35:37,260 --> 00:35:42,420
The plant has been jokingly described as a multimillion-dollar garbage disposal for reactors,

388
00:35:42,880 --> 00:35:46,860
but this tremendous and vital job is no joke.

389
00:35:47,940 --> 00:35:51,519
For every one pound of uranium-235 recovered,

390
00:35:52,059 --> 00:35:55,639
the plant ends up with 1,000 pounds of liquid wastes.

391
00:35:56,440 --> 00:36:01,619
What can you do with a steady output of thousands of gallons of radioactive and corrosive liquids

392
00:36:01,619 --> 00:36:06,119
that you can't throw out or even allow to leak out for the next several hundred years.

393
00:36:07,019 --> 00:36:12,539
We have been safely storing these wastes in very expensive stainless steel underground tanks

394
00:36:12,539 --> 00:36:14,799
surrounded by concrete vaults.

395
00:36:14,799 --> 00:36:19,380
But this is not a very practical long-term solution to this difficult problem,

396
00:36:20,019 --> 00:36:24,239
particularly since these tanks cannot be considered permanently leak-proof.

397
00:36:25,639 --> 00:36:30,679
One of the most encouraging advances to date is demonstrated in successful operation

398
00:36:30,679 --> 00:36:34,719
of the Waste Calcining Facility, or WCF,

399
00:36:35,279 --> 00:36:41,739
the world's first unit for plant-scale conversion of highly radioactive liquid wastes to solid form.

400
00:36:42,599 --> 00:36:46,399
In the WCF process, the waste solution is sprayed

401
00:36:46,399 --> 00:36:50,819
into a heated bed of solid particles, fluidized by blasts of air,

402
00:36:51,539 --> 00:36:55,139
leaving a calcined residue of small, dry granules

403
00:36:55,139 --> 00:36:57,739
that takes up only a tenth as much space.

404
00:36:57,740 --> 00:37:09,340
A continuous air stream blows the excess granules through underground tubes and into buried storage bins of less costly construction than liquid storage tanks.

405
00:37:10,780 --> 00:37:15,000
The stored granules won't seep or leak and are non-corrosive.

406
00:37:16,300 --> 00:37:23,400
Every 200 days the plant operates, it calcines the contents of a $1 million liquid storage tank.

407
00:37:23,400 --> 00:37:33,400
Less costly management of liquid wastes is one of the problems that must be solved before we can obtain economic nuclear power in all areas.

408
00:37:33,400 --> 00:37:41,400
And it is just one of the research jobs of the huge engineering laboratory that we call the National Reactor Testing Station.

409
00:37:42,400 --> 00:37:49,400
The last two reactors on our rounds are quite different in many respects from the others we've seen.

410
00:37:49,400 --> 00:37:51,400
And totally different in one momentous way.

411
00:37:51,400 --> 00:37:55,400
they can breed more new fuel than they consume,

412
00:37:55,400 --> 00:38:01,400
a phenomenon of obvious importance to the long-term future of atomic energy.

413
00:38:01,400 --> 00:38:05,400
The pioneer in the new direction was EBR-1,

414
00:38:05,400 --> 00:38:08,400
Experimental Breeder Reactor Number One,

415
00:38:08,400 --> 00:38:12,400
the world's first reactor to demonstrate the breeding principle

416
00:38:12,400 --> 00:38:15,400
and to produce useful amounts of electricity.

417
00:38:15,400 --> 00:38:18,400
A follow-on reactor of the same family

418
00:38:18,400 --> 00:38:21,579
of the same family, is Experimental Breeder Reactor

419
00:38:21,579 --> 00:38:25,960
Number Two, a key facility in the AEC's program

420
00:38:25,960 --> 00:38:29,079
for meeting long-range energy needs.

421
00:38:29,079 --> 00:38:33,519
Like its predecessor, EBR-2 is a fast reactor.

422
00:38:33,519 --> 00:38:36,639
That is, it contains no moderating material.

423
00:38:36,639 --> 00:38:43,200
The nuclear fissions in its fuel are caused by neutrons traveling about 13,000 miles per second,

424
00:38:43,200 --> 00:38:51,260
compared to one mile per second when slowed down by passage through a moderator such as graphite or water.

425
00:38:51,260 --> 00:38:58,160
Using non-moderating liquid metal to carry heat to its steam generating plants, EBR2

426
00:38:58,160 --> 00:39:03,220
operates at high temperature for efficient power production and low pressure for safer

427
00:39:03,220 --> 00:39:06,260
and potentially more economic operation.

428
00:39:06,260 --> 00:39:15,440
However, the main distinction is that EBR2 can manufacture more fuel than it burns.

429
00:39:15,440 --> 00:39:22,260
You'll remember that when a neutron hits an atom of U-235, it will probably fission it.

430
00:39:22,260 --> 00:39:29,420
If a neutron hits a U-238 atom, it will most likely bounce off or be absorbed without causing

431
00:39:29,420 --> 00:39:31,100
fission.

432
00:39:31,099 --> 00:39:38,440
If the neutron is absorbed, it is not wasted because the U-238 atom that swallows it turns

433
00:39:38,440 --> 00:39:48,099
into Neptunium and then into Plutonium, Plutonium-239, a fine reactor fuel in its own right and superior

434
00:39:48,099 --> 00:39:52,719
to U-235 in fast breeder reactors.

435
00:39:52,719 --> 00:39:57,900
The significance of fast reactors in this connection is that their high-speed neutrons

436
00:39:57,900 --> 00:40:03,940
produce more new neutrons per reaction than we get out of reactions caused by the slow

437
00:40:03,940 --> 00:40:11,660
neutrons in our moderated reactors. Therefore, the fast reactors have more spare neutrons

438
00:40:11,660 --> 00:40:17,240
than the moderated reactors to apply to the job of breeding new plutonium fuel from the

439
00:40:17,240 --> 00:40:24,500
relatively inert U-238. Also, fewer of these spare neutrons are wasted by absorption into

440
00:40:24,500 --> 00:40:32,659
structural materials. When the EBR-1 first tested our theories about the breeding of plutonium in

441
00:40:32,659 --> 00:40:40,579
1952, its fuel elements were surrounded by a breeding blanket of natural uranium. After a

442
00:40:40,579 --> 00:40:46,739
period of reactor operation, the breeding blanket was analyzed and it was confirmed that for every

443
00:40:46,740 --> 00:40:53,680
atom of U-235 consumed, a little more than one new atom of plutonium had been

444
00:40:53,680 --> 00:40:58,380
created. Plutonium which could be extracted from the breeding blanket and

445
00:40:58,380 --> 00:41:05,520
used in new fuel elements in the place of U-235. This was the result that told

446
00:41:05,520 --> 00:41:10,460
us we can burn substantially all of the uranium we can find instead of just 7

447
00:41:10,460 --> 00:41:15,519
tenths of one percent if we learn to build the right kind of reactors.

448
00:41:15,519 --> 00:41:21,460
Otherwise, as soon as we burn up the tiny fraction of U-235 in our natural uranium

449
00:41:21,460 --> 00:41:27,980
we would end up with mountains of U-238 that we could never burn, totally useless

450
00:41:27,980 --> 00:41:34,940
for power. By perfecting the fuel breeding process, however, we make

451
00:41:34,940 --> 00:41:40,360
available an almost inexhaustible supply of energy more than 2,000 times the

452
00:41:40,360 --> 00:41:44,700
energy contained in all our remaining fossil fuels.

453
00:41:44,700 --> 00:41:47,360
The benefits of fuel breeding go still further.

454
00:41:47,360 --> 00:41:51,200
They promise us power from supplies of the element thorium.

455
00:41:51,200 --> 00:41:56,079
When an atom of this metal in the breeding blanket of a reactor absorbs a neutron, it

456
00:41:56,079 --> 00:42:00,200
is transmuted into unstable thorium-233.

457
00:42:00,200 --> 00:42:06,860
It then decays to protactinium-233 and then to uranium-233.

458
00:42:06,860 --> 00:42:12,740
Like plutonium, this new element, uranium-233, is fissionable and another potentially good

459
00:42:12,740 --> 00:42:15,820
nuclear fuel.

460
00:42:15,820 --> 00:42:20,640
The engineering problems that must be solved before our reactors are completely effective

461
00:42:20,640 --> 00:42:26,380
for both breeding and power production are complex, enormous.

462
00:42:26,380 --> 00:42:32,180
It may take decades, but success will be rewarding beyond measure.

463
00:42:32,179 --> 00:42:37,460
able to breed new fuel as they burn the old, while turning out their enormous

464
00:42:37,460 --> 00:42:43,460
floods of power, could supply our major energy needs for centuries to come. We

465
00:42:43,460 --> 00:42:50,839
could both have our cake and eat it. EBR2's job is to develop plutonium fuels

466
00:42:50,839 --> 00:42:56,599
for use in future power reactors and to advance the fast breeder technology in

467
00:42:56,599 --> 00:43:04,940
in general. Integral to the operation of EBR-2 is the fuel cycle facility. First plant-scale

468
00:43:04,940 --> 00:43:14,380
attempt to melt, refine, and refabricate discharged fuel elements on the spot, all by remote control.

469
00:43:14,380 --> 00:43:20,639
Pilot techniques have demonstrated how EBR-2 spent fuel elements can be extracted from

470
00:43:20,639 --> 00:43:29,119
reactor core, melted down and purified. Then recast into completely new fuel pins entirely

471
00:43:29,119 --> 00:43:36,239
by remote processes operating behind thick shielding windows. This integrated pyrometallurgical

472
00:43:36,239 --> 00:43:42,079
processing could reduce capital costs by eliminating delays of up to six months

473
00:43:42,079 --> 00:43:48,799
occasioned by conventional processing and refabrication. It is one more complex but

474
00:43:48,800 --> 00:43:55,340
important step in the assignment that keeps NRTS so endlessly busy. The mandate

475
00:43:55,340 --> 00:44:00,500
to develop and test practical sources of the atomic power needed to keep our

476
00:44:00,500 --> 00:44:06,920
civilization healthy and its future secure. Not enough power for all time nor

477
00:44:06,920 --> 00:44:13,000
for all needs, but a bridge to the day when we contain the energy of the Sun by

478
00:44:13,000 --> 00:44:19,039
mastering the thermonuclear sources of its fire in fusion reactors.

479
00:44:19,039 --> 00:44:24,159
And their prototypes may first appear right here at the National Reactor Testing Station

480
00:44:24,159 --> 00:44:33,659
in Idaho, where so many earlier plants have grown from theory to demonstration.

481
00:44:33,659 --> 00:44:38,920
This is the home of the new power, the power of tomorrow.
